[{:seon.task/name "write-back-answers-what-happened"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.fx/write-file` writes path.fx-PID and renames it over path with rename(2) (`seon.lib/c-rename`, with errno): a failed rename answers :seon.fx/io-error {:path :tmp :errno :message} and removes the tmp; success answers {:value {:bytes n :path path}}, n the path's size on disk after the rename. Replaces an ignored `.renameTo` boolean, which answered success for a rename that failed (a missing directory, a cross-device path, a permission) and left the tmp, so the loop's last step (merged code written back) could record a write that never reached disk. One syscall more; constant, no cost proof."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-failed-rename-is-an-io-error-and-leaves-no-tmp
                      seon.fx-test/a-write-back-answers-the-bytes-on-disk}}
 {:seon.task/name "merge-trains-off-as-decided"
  :seon.task/plan "Restates a ruling now in force (DECISIONS: Root's calls on R3's questions: octopus merges stay off until one combined test run of all members, the par-200 hang and root memory growth with concurrent merges are fixed): `:seon.config.merge/max-running` is 1 in resources/seon/config/default.edn, read through the one resolver, and `seon.task/boot-tx` writes it on main with the config in force; the code default `seon.reconcile/default-max-merging` is deleted. Nothing left to do: close."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/boot-records-merge-max-running-one
                      seon.reconcile-test/two-ready-disjoint-merges-run-one-at-a-time-by-default}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-transient-model-failure-parks"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: a :seon.model/rate-limited outcome (the provider's 429) is not answered to its asker: `seon.fx/owner-proc` records the :effect problem, `seon.fx/settle` halves the bucket's rate (AIMD), and the request the worker performed goes back on the queue under its old key, so `seon.fx/dispatch` sends it again when the bucket refills; the agent's fx! stays parked on its one answer, the call that went through or a non-transient error. This is the delayed insert AGENTS.md section 4 allows for a provider's retry-after. A transport failure (`seon.model/http-post`'s caught exception, no status) is not transient by any provider's statement: it answers the agent and ends the attempt :error, recorded; re-sending it would be a blind retry. `seon.model/http-post` has no deadline (the reply ends when the server ends it). Per 429: one queue insert, and no model call beyond those the provider refused."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-admit-test/a-rate-limited-model-call-waits-for-the-bucket-and-is-answered}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-test-bound-derived-and-named"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: a test run is bounded by its work, never a clock: `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` runs the proofs within `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` engine ticks (named in docs/failure-points.md; bin/check robust asserts that a test that never ends is stopped with :seon.test/ticks-spent naming it). The child writes {:seon.test/started var} as each test var begins (clojure.test's :begin-test-var report, which Jolt emits) and its result as the last line; `seon.agent/run-answer` turns the last line and the exit status into the run: the result, or one :error report on the started var with :exit, so `seon.proofs/results` reads that test :error and the context shows a red proof with the death beside it. Deleted: the 30000 ms literals at 8 sites, the :timeout-ms forwarding in `seon.root/ext-op`, the deadline loop of run-tests-forked, and `seon.root/fs-call!`'s 30000 ms deref fallback. The ticks are not a clock and stay: without them robust's endless test hangs. A run that hangs otherwise ends only by an explicit kill of the agent (a stall is task seon.fences/a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound). v1 bounded each test by a declared per-test bound (default 5 s, seon.test/bounded-result, ~/src/seon)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-killed-test-run-names-the-test-that-was-running
                      seon.web-test/calls-become-requests}}
 {:seon.task/name "records-keep-the-exact-value"
  :seon.task/plan "Storage keeps the exact value; renders budget it (AGENTS.md section 9: a clipping function). Left in src: `seon.proofs/failures` keeps the first `seon.proofs/max-assertions` failing assertions per test and drops the rest with no count; seon.root records a dead agent's stderr as its last tail-bytes (2048) though the file (`seon.agent/stderr-path`) is kept beside the store. Gone already: `seon.agent/error-map`'s message-chars cut, seon.model's 300-char HTTP error body, seon.learn's 80-char form and seon.agent-proc's 200-char form in :last-op and :running. Correct: each record holds the whole value or a reference to it (the stderr file's path and size; all assertions, or the first n plus :omitted n and the run's handle); the context and the dashboard render them under their budgets through the one printer. Deletes max-assertions and tail-bytes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-exception-message-is-recorded-whole
                      seon.proofs-test/every-failing-assertion-is-kept-or-counted
                      seon.problems-test/a-death-record-points-at-the-whole-stderr}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-boots-without-a-synthetic-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.root/start!` seeds no program by default (:datoms 0), and boot no longer depends on a seed (the warm-up that queried app.m0/f1 is gone). `seon.db/seed-tx` (18 namespaces app.m0..app.m17 of 20 uncontracted defns each, with held app.mN/tidy tasks) is written only where a check asks for it (`seon.check`'s smoke, robust and scale pass :datoms 2000). Before, start! defaulted to :datoms 2000, so the standing root and every check forked agents holding a program nobody wrote."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.main-test/a-fresh-root-holds-no-program-it-was-not-given
                      seon.main-test/warm-needs-no-seed}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-compute-executor-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "Jolt's Flow runs :compute steps on `clojure.core.async.impl.dispatch`'s compute-executor (reference-code/jolt/stdlib/clojure/core/async/impl/dispatch.clj:60), an unbounded newCachedThreadPool of unnamed daemon threads with a 60 s keep-alive, and bounds each step with .get compute-timeout-ms 5000 (flow/impl.clj:419): a step past 5 s throws into root's errors while its worker keeps running, and the next step takes another worker. No proc in src declares :workload :compute today (`seon.render.proc/render-proc` is :mixed), so the hypothesis that it explains root's unattributed threads (lifecycle 25 -> 29 over cycles, scale 100 root threads 38 -> 55) is unproven. Correct: first prove or refute it (name the threads, or count compute workers before and after each cycle). If it holds, root passes create-flow a declared :compute-exec (a fixed pool of named threads, its size a seon.config key) and a step's bound becomes an observed overrun (its duration recorded), not an abandoned future. Estimate: root's thread count after a cycle equals the declared sum, exactly."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares
                      seon.render-test/a-slow-render-step-is-recorded-not-abandoned}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-is-answered-and-failover-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "(1) Done: `seon.root/agent-health` and `seon.root/health`, which answered :unknown past 1000 ms, are deleted (task seon.check/every-wait-is-the-event-it-means); `seon.check`'s one ping records a proc that does not answer as :blocked. (2) Open: the fork server's :standby-failed event (`seon.fork-server`, forwarded to the api) reaches `seon.root/back-op`, whose :ev case has no branch for it and falls through to the unchanged state: dropped. The server then has no standby, and its next death is :fork-server-lost with no recorded cause. Correct: a ping's answer passes through as itself (an error map stays an error map); :unknown only for a proc observed dead; a failed standby is a recorded platform problem (:seon.fork-server/standby-failed with :active-threads), and the view shows the server has no standby."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-ping-of-a-gone-agent-answers-its-error
                      seon.procs-test/a-failed-standby-is-a-recorded-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "leases-and-store-size-are-declared-bounds"
  :seon.task/plan "Part (1) replaced 2026-10-23 by store-wide ids (task seon.db/each-id-is-issued-once): no agent holds a block, so none can mint past one. Done: an agent's lease is a ceiling where the id is minted, on our jolt/eid-lease Datahike branch: `seon.db/lease!` (from `seon.db/after-fork!`) sets a per-store ceiling with `dbtx/set-eid-ceiling!` at lease + `:seon.config.store/eid-lease`, and next-eid raises {:error :entity-id/lease-spent} past it, before anything applies (`dw/apply!` runs ahead, so a check after it would be too late); `seon.db/lease-spent` turns that into the typed :seon.db/lease-spent naming the lease and the fix, as an :err item in the agent's eval. The LMDB map size is `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes` in resources/seon/config/default.edn (the SEON_LMDB_MB, SEON_TRIP_MB and SEON_AGENT_HEAP_MB reads are gone), and the store reports its map use (`seon.db/map-use`: data.mdb's length against the map size; `seon.db/lease-use` an agent's ids used of its lease). Before, an agent minting past its block silently took the next agent's ids, and two branches merged different entities under one id."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/each-id-is-issued-once-across-branches
                      seon.db-test/the-store-reports-its-map-use}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-ends-on-a-defect-too"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.e2e/await-quiet` completes on main's quiescence fact or on a root proc's defect recorded on main after t, answering {:ended :defect :problem p}, which the run prints and fails on; no clock. Before, it waited on quiescence alone, so a flight whose answer was lost (a reconciler or api step throws: Flow keeps the old state and drops its outputs) left the run and bin/check hanging with nothing recorded. Once seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took merges this path should not fire; it stays as the observation."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/await-quiet-ends-on-a-recorded-defect}}
 {:seon.task/name "checks-await-facts-and-assert-exact-numbers"
  :seon.task/plan "What remains in `seon.check`: five Thread/sleep windows (the stall provoked by `(Thread/sleep 2500)` in a submitted form, and sleeps of `:seon.config.check/pages-ms`, `:seon.config.check/hold-ms` twice and window-ms), bin/check orphan polling kill -0 once a second, and tolerances that are guesses (robust's printed values < 25000 chars, lifecycle's thread slack, backpressure's arrival ceiling). Correct: readiness and ends are facts (tap c/events for :ready and :dead, `seon.e2e/await-db` on main), a paused proc's state is its ping; orphans' deaths are kqueue EVFILT_PROC events; the stall is a form parked on a never-answered channel; each number is exact: threads equal root's declared count (task seon.root/root-threads-are-a-known-number), backpressure's ceiling is the agent buffer + the end's :in + the frames the 1 MB kernel buffer holds + one per pump (frame bytes from seon.sock/frame), robust asserts the printer's :tokens within the reply budget. Each check prints its derived number beside the measured one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.check/branches-as-in-production" "seon.root/root-threads-are-a-known-number"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/no-check-sleeps-or-polls
                      seon.check-test/backpressure-asserts-its-derived-ceiling}}
 {:seon.task/name "cost-tolerances-are-derived"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.cost/slack` {:ns 3.0 :bytes 1.25 :calls 1.0} (bytes called exact, yet 25% because the unit table flakes, task seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache: 11.6 vs 8.9 KB; ns 3x for load), `seon.cost/steeper-by` 0.3 and `seon.cost/ratchet` 1.5 are guesses; test/seon/profile_test.clj asserts within 2x and only when contracts are on (zero assertions when off). Correct: each unit row records its spread over k runs under a declared cache state; a dimension's slack is the spread of the units its estimate prices (bytes then near 1.0); ns carries the run's load and its spread at that load; the exponent tolerance is derived per dimension and proof as log(slack_d)/log(n_max/n_min) (over 100x: bytes 1.25 -> 0.048, ns 3 -> 0.24); the ratchet is the overrun's own measured spread at filing; every verdict prints the derived tolerances, and the agent's prompt renders them from seon.cost; profile_test asserts the sampling rule exactly (ceil(n/64) timed calls) with contracts on. Tried: jolt/unitflake (2838e62..cc43b5b; retargeted diff next/notes/archive/worktrees/unitflake-retargeted-2026-10-22.diff, applies on 5af7b4b); not merged because on main it turns three green cost proofs red, so the derivation is wrong: the calls exponent tolerance derives to 0.000 (every unit row's calls range is exactly 1.0) and a fixed per-call overhead bends a linear fit (`seon.docs-cost-test/an-unchanged-reload-costs-a-parse-and-a-pull-per-section` n^1.01, `seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass` n^1.03), and the ratchet tightened to 1.26x puts `seon.program-cost-test/recording-a-form-is-flat-in-the-program` past its open overrun. The plan owes: an exponent tolerance that holds for exact dimensions (from the fit's residual, or the estimate's fixed term), the ratchet's rule stated, then the unit table re-measured with seon.cost-test/table (:pr-str/char measures 33.05 B against the row's 29.36)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/the-exponent-tolerance-follows-slack-and-span
                      seon.cost-test/slack-is-the-units-measured-spread
                      seon.context-test/the-prompt-states-the-judges-own-tolerances}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-dragon-gate-sees-fences"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: bin/laws lists the sites of each law (catch against docs/failure-points.md; clocks, a sleep, timeout, deadline or *-MS, against docs/clocks.md; measures; resolution) by matching marks in code text with strings and comments stripped (awk), and is red on a site whose function the law's page does not name. Wrong: matching text cannot see a failure turned into a value through a resolved var it does not spell (deref with 3 args, alts! against a timeout, `clojure.core.async.flow/ping-proc` with a bound, Thread .join with a bound), and regex over code stands in for the compiler's facts (AGENTS.md section 9). Correct: the gate reads the compiler's resolved references per definition (the mechanism seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler records, the same one the intern and alter-var-root sites use), lists every call of clojure.core/deref with 3 args, java.lang.Thread/sleep, clojure.core.async/timeout, clojure.core.async.flow/ping-proc, Thread .join and catch, in src and in the check drivers, and is red unless docs/failure-points.md names that function's row (a real failure point with its declared answer). Replaces the awk; the page gains a Guards section."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/an-unnamed-timeout-fallback-is-red
                      seon.check-test/the-laws-check-reads-resolved-references}}
 {:seon.task/name "no-clip-in-renders"
  :seon.task/plan "Done in part: `seon.render.ui/clip` and line, seon.render.ai's char cuts and its 4-chars-a-token budget are gone, and the render proc's event, series and history caps are config keys (resources/seon/config/default.edn, provenance EVENT-CAP 60, SERIES-CAP 60, HISTORY-CAP 100). Left: the api's view keeps :evals and :root-errors with `(take-last 20 ...)` in seon.root, a list dropped silently (AGENTS.md section 9: a clipping function). Correct: each capped list says how many more and links to the record on main; each clipped value gets a :seon.render/ai or :seon.render/html render on its schema, or goes through the one printer with a handle; budgets are :seon.render/budget on the view schemas, measured by the printer's own token count."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/no-render-calls-clip
                      seon.render-test/a-capped-list-says-how-many-more}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.agent-proc/stall-ms` (30000, a guessed tolerance kept as a var, not a config key) is the silence after which a busy agent is observed stalled once; `seon.root/start!` takes :stall-ms, and the api's step schedules its stall check with a go block on `(a/timeout (quot stall-ms 2))` in seon.root. A 25 s compute shows nothing while a legitimate long proof run reads as stalled. Correct: a request carries its declared bound (the form's, task seon.turn/a-form-has-a-time-bound; a test run's is its tick budget, `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks`) and the agent proc observes a stall when that bound passes with no frame: one recorded :seon.agent/stalled naming the bound, no second number; the ticks come from the one :clock proc (task seon.root/one-clock). Deletes stall-ms, the :stall-ms option and the go-block timer. The flusher's sleep this task also named is gone (durability is continuous group commit, task seon.writer/durability-is-continuous-group-commit)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/one-clock" "seon.turn/a-form-has-a-time-bound" "seon.fences/one-test-bound-derived-and-named"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-stall-is-observed-past-the-requests-own-bound
                      seon.procs-test/the-flusher-waits-on-the-clock}}
 {:seon.task/name "event-loss-is-counted"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's buffers are literals: `seon.root/start!`'s events-in is `(a/sliding-buffer 8192)`, `seon.root/events` taps a sliding 1024 that is never untapped, and the render proc's tap is a sliding 4096 (seon.render.proc). A sliding buffer drops the oldest with no count, so the dashboard can miss events and nothing says so. Correct (the step-fn law: buffers bounded by their loss semantics): every buffer is declared in resources/seon/system.edn :chan-opts with its loss; a sliding buffer counts its drops into the api's view (:events-dropped by tap), shown on the dashboard; c/events taps are released with their reader. Estimate: one counter per drop."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-dropped-event-is-counted
                      seon.procs-test/root-buffers-come-from-the-declaration}}
 {:seon.task/name "fork-refusal-names-the-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Done in part: `seon.lib/fork!` forks only while this is the one active Chez thread and otherwise calls its :refused fn at once with the active thread count, so a test run beside a thread answers :seon.test/fork-refused {:active-threads n} (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`) instead of the old safe-fork's 1 s spin. Left: `seon.agent`'s interrupt-self-test spins up to 100,000 evals waiting for its own SIGUSR1. Correct: the self-test waits on one safe point (one engine tick) and records whether the handler ran. Deletes the 100,000. Its two proofs are not yet written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-fork-beside-a-thread-is-refused-at-once-naming-it
                      seon.agent-test/the-interrupt-self-test-needs-one-safe-point}}]
